mk("yZjQ2e4IREI","Step-by-Step Guide: Beautiful Wavetable Pads (Ableton Live 12)",
 "macro-randomizable-evolving-wavetable-pad-two-formant-wt-oscs-minus-1-and-2-oct-serial-12db-lp-hp-macro-env2-to-wt-positions-env3-to-env2-attack-external-triangle-lfo-opposing-adsr-offsets-unison-shimmer",
 "Self-evolving wavetable pad rack you can safely randomize: two formant-style wavetable oscillators (-1 and -2 octaves) through serial 12 dB low-pass and high-pass filters on one macro, an internal matrix where envelope 2 drives both wavetable positions and envelope 3 drives envelope 2's attack, plus an external slow triangle LFO that pushes the amp envelope times in opposite directions, with ADSR excluded from macro randomization",
 "Build a lush, gently moving pad as a macro rack. Oscillators: osc 1 and osc 2 on formant/voice-type wavetables, osc 1 at -1 octave and osc 2 at -2 octaves, sub on, volume about -12 dB, unison 'shimmer' mode with 4 voices. Filters: two 12 dB filters in serial (low-pass and high-pass) with about 2 dB and 1 dB drive and one shared frequency macro so raising it also trims lows, and one shared resonance macro kept low. Internal mod matrix: env 2 to osc 1 position 50 and to osc 2 position 75; LFO 1 to each position 15; env 2 to filter 1 frequency 10 and LFO 1 to it 3; env 3 to env 2 attack 100; LFO 1 to env 2 sustain 50; env 2 to filter 2 frequency -2; LFO 1 retrigger off, very slow. External LFO (triangle, low Hz) after the synth: filter frequency 50, amp attack -60, decay +30, sustain -40, release +40 (opposing directions for complexity), osc 1 warp 50, osc 1 fold 20, osc 2 effect 50. Macros: positions, warp/fold, osc 2 transpose, filter frequency, resonance, ADSR (amp + env 2 + env 3 together), mod rate (both LFOs), mod amount, LFO shape, shimmer amount. Add a limiter last. Exclude the ADSR macros from randomize, then randomize and store snapshots.",
 ["A wavetable synth with at least 3 envelopes, 1-2 LFOs, a mod matrix that can target envelope times, warp/fold oscillator effects and serial filters","A macro/rack system or parameter-mapping layer with snapshots and randomize","An external LFO/modulator device and a limiter"],
 [("wavetables","string","Two soft formant/voice-like wavetables (original or free)",True),("note_range","string","Pad register; oscillators sit at -1 and -2 octaves",False)],
 [("pad_rack","synth-patch","16-macro evolving pad rack"),("snapshots","preset-set","Stored randomized variations")],
 ["Matrix and LFO amounts are the speaker's values on a 0-100 scale; treat them as relative depths when porting.",
  "Keep LFO rates very low and resonance low: the video stresses that pads do not need rapid modulation. No pitch modulation is used, which suits Matt's preference for steady pitch.",
  "The external LFO modulation is not saved inside a synth preset; store the whole rack with snapshots, or in Web Audio keep the LFO in the patch graph.",
  "Excluding ADSR from randomize keeps the pad's duration/sustain character while timbre is explored.",A],
 [("Create a rack on a MIDI track and expose all 16 macros; insert the wavetable synth.","Instrument rack","An empty mappable pad shell."),
  ("Osc 1: formant/voice wavetable at -1 octave; enable osc 2 with a darker formant wavetable at -2 octaves; enable the sub; set synth volume to about -12 dB; unison mode 'shimmer' with 4 voices.","Wavetable oscillators","A deep, breathy two-oscillator tone."),
  ("Enable both filters as 12 dB types in serial (low-pass then high-pass), drive about 2 dB and 1 dB.","Filters","A warm band that can be thinned from below."),
  ("Matrix: env 2 → osc 1 position 50, osc 2 position 75; LFO 1 → both positions 15; env 2 → filter 1 freq 10; LFO 1 → filter 1 freq 3; env 3 → env 2 attack 100; LFO 1 → env 2 sustain 50; env 2 → filter 2 freq -2. LFO 1: sine, retrigger off, slow.","Mod matrix","Each note's timbre moves on its own and differs note to note."),
  ("Map macros: osc 1 position, osc 1 warp, osc 1 fold, osc 2 position, osc 2 effect, osc 2 transpose, filter frequency (both filters on one knob), resonance (both), amp/env 2/env 3 ADSR, mod rate (LFO rate), mod amount, mod shape, shimmer amount.","Macro mapping","All key controls on one page."),
  ("Add an external LFO after the synth, set to triangle at a low Hz rate; map its rate/depth to the mod-rate/mod-amount macros. Map it to filter frequency (50), amp attack (-60), decay (+30), sustain (-40), release (+40), osc 1 warp (50), osc 1 fold (20), osc 2 effect (50).","External LFO device","Envelope times and wave shapes drift in opposing directions over time."),
  ("Add a limiter last as a safety against level jumps.","Limiter","Output never spikes during randomizing."),
  ("Exclude the ADSR macros from randomization, press randomize until a pad you like appears, and save each as a snapshot; save the rack.","Macro randomize + snapshots","A bank of related, stable-length pad variations.")],
 ["Holding one chord for 30 s yields audible timbre motion without pitch change.","Randomizing macros never changes the amp ADSR values.","Raising the filter macro brightens the top and trims the low end at the same time."],
 ["pad","wavetable","mod-matrix","macro-rack","randomize","evolving","formant","modulation"],A)

mk("mnledZoD4QE","How to make an ambient pad in Logic Pro X - Trance Production Tutorials #10",
 "fully-wet-extreme-reverb-pad-from-percussive-sources-20-40s-chained-reverbs-bitcrush-pre-reverb-for-sustained-harmonics-eq-tame-layered-epiano-vibes-piano-glock-bus-low-cut-sidechain-filter-reveal",
 "Turn bright percussive or decaying instruments (electric piano, vibraphone, piano, glockenspiel) into an evolving ambient pad by running each through 100% wet reverbs of about 20-40 s (chaining two reverbs for longer tails), bit-crushing the piano before the reverb to sustain and add harmonics, taming harsh highs with EQ, then busing the layers with a low cut, compression, sidechain and a filter that reveals the pad",
 "Make an ambient pad from stock instruments rather than a synth. Pick sources with rich, bright harmonics (distorted electric piano, vibraphone, piano, glockenspiel) and play the same chords stacked across several octaves, panning some layers slightly left and right. Put each through a reverb set to fully wet with no dry signal and a very long decay (about 33 s, 22 s, 40 s in the video); daisy-chain two algorithmic reverbs to get even longer tails and lower the level. The reverb smears the transients away and effectively adds a long attack. For extra brightness and sustain, bit-crush (distort) the piano before its reverb, then EQ down the harsh upper-mid/top it added. Roll off some highs on very bright sources like glockenspiel before the reverb. Because the layers use different reverb lengths they create multiple attack phases, so the sound evolves. Bus all layers; EQ out the large low-frequency/sub-20 Hz build-up from the long reverbs, add compression, sidechain compression and a low-pass filter that you automate open to introduce the pad.",
 ["A DAW with buses and automation","Two or more reverbs with wet/dry control and decay above 20 s","A bit crusher or distortion, EQ, compressor with sidechain, and a filter","Instrument sounds: electric piano, vibraphone, piano, glockenspiel or any bright, harmonic source"],
 [("chords","midi","Chords stacked across several octaves",True),("sources","list","Bright, harmonically rich instruments",True),("reverb_decays_s","list","Per-layer decay, about 20-40 s",False)],
 [("pad_bus","audio-bus","Layered fully wet reverb pad"),("pad_audio","audio","Rendered pad for reuse")],
 ["Any reverb works as long as it has a wet/dry control and a very long decay; convolution and algorithmic both shown.",
  "Always check the low end: extreme reverbs build up a lot of low and sub-20 Hz energy that must be cut on the bus.",
  "Bit-crush adds harsh highs; follow it with EQ cuts so the pad is not fatiguing.",
  "Brightness lever for Matt's palette: the bit-crush/distortion before reverb is the harmonic exciter step; dial it to taste rather than boosting EQ.",A],
 [("Program a couple of chords stacked across several octaves; load bright sources (distorted electric piano, vibraphone, piano, glockenspiel) on separate tracks, panning a few slightly left or right.","DAW + instruments","Dry, plucky layers with lots of high harmonics."),
  ("On the electric piano, insert a reverb at 100% wet / 0% dry with about 33 s decay.","Reverb","The quick attack becomes a slow swell; it reads as a pad."),
  ("On the vibraphone, daisy-chain two algorithmic reverbs (fully wet) for an even longer tail; turn the track down.","Two reverbs in series","An ethereal sustained tone where the source is no longer recognisable."),
  ("On the piano, insert bit crusher → EQ → reverb (fully wet, about 22 s); crush fairly hard to sustain and add harmonics, then cut the harsh upper-mid/top with EQ.","Bit crusher, EQ, reverb","Louder sustained harmonics feeding the reverb; a brighter, less fatiguing texture."),
  ("On the glockenspiel, roll off some of the top with EQ before a fully wet reverb of about 40 s (chained reverbs).","EQ + reverbs","A shimmering but not piercing high layer."),
  ("Listen to all layers together: different decays give multiple attack phases that morph over time.","Mixer","A pad that evolves dynamically compared with the dry chord."),
  ("Route all layers to a bus: EQ out the low/sub build-up, add compression and sidechain compression, and a low-pass filter.","Bus processing","A controlled, pumping-ready pad bus."),
  ("Automate the bus filter open to introduce the pad in the arrangement.","Filter automation","The atmosphere rises in rather than appearing abruptly.")],
 ["Each source track has its reverb at 100% wet and decay of 20 s or more.","A spectrum analyser shows the bus low cut removing sub-20 Hz build-up.","The dry chord and processed pad are clearly different in attack (pad swells in over seconds)."],
 ["pad","reverb","ambient","percussive-to-pad","bit-crush","brightness","layering","atmosphere"],A)

mk("axLAIw6Iw7A","Arrangement Hacks for MELODIC TECHNO | Jonas Saalbach | Ableton Live",
 "withhold-the-reveal-arrangement-constant-ostinato-and-one-note-bass-until-stacked-firsts-climax-pad-minus-14db-in-full-parts-fx-marked-entries-silence-before-mutes-loopable-edges",
 "Arrangement that saves several 'firsts' for one climactic moment: keep a three-note ostinato unchanged from the start to that moment, keep the bass on one note until then, and change the ostinato, start the moving bassline and bring in a new percussion layer and the lead together; pull the constant pad from about -3 dB to about -14 dB in full sections, mark every entry or exit with a small FX hit, put a short silence before big mutes, and keep intro and outro loopable",
 "Delta vs the existing loop-is-the-climax and four-levels arrangement skills: those reveal the loop gradually; this one holds one element deliberately unchanged and stacks several first-time changes into one moment. Structure from the video: loopable intro (no fade noise across the loop point); the arpeggio/ostinato and a filtered kick from the start; a feedback drone building; around 1 minute in, bassline, snare and the rest come together; take your time with the build-up but not 2 minutes; a short break optional; faster hats and opening the arp filter for an early peak; low-cut the bass to put focus on the arp. In the drop keep the bass on one note. In full sections check each channel and mute what is not needed; turn the constant pad down from about -3 dB to about -14 dB. Mark each entry or exit with small FX or drum fills (orchestral hits in the intro). After about 3.5-4 minutes, change the arp for the first time at the same moment the moving bassline enters, a new off-beat shaker enters, the lead enters and the pad plays the arp's harmony. Then the drop: the bass stops briefly while the hats keep running; every 8 bars switch one element. Put a quick moment of silence before muting many channels. Build down slowly and keep the outro loopable but not overly long for album versions.",
 ["A DAW arrangement with gain automation and markers","A loop with an ostinato/arp, bass, pad, percussion and a lead"],
 [("ostinato","midi","A short repeating figure (three notes in the video)",True),("reveal_time","string","Where the big first-time change lands (about 3.5-4 min in a 6-8 min track)",True),("track_length_min","number","Target length",False)],
 [("arrangement","daw-arrangement","Arrangement with one stacked reveal moment"),("reveal_checklist","text","List of elements held back and released at the reveal")],
 ["Melodic-techno timings (1 min build, 4 min reveal) scale down for shorter ambient/cinematic cues: keep the ratio (reveal at about 55-65% of the length).",
  "Matt prefers drums entering early: start the kick (filtered) from bar 1 as in the video rather than a long beatless intro.",
  "The held ostinato must stay rhythmically and melodically steady until the reveal; no random variation.",
  "Specific plug-ins and presets in the video are not used; any synth or drum source works.",A],
 [("Make the intro loopable: no fade-in noise or tails across the loop point so it can be repeated or mixed.","Arrangement","A clean loopable opening."),
  ("Start the ostinato and a filtered kick from the beginning; add a building drone; bring in bassline, snare and other drums around the 1-minute mark (build-up shorter than about 2 minutes).","Arrangement + filter automation","Steady pulse early, a clear build."),
  ("Write the held-back list: ostinato change, moving bassline, a new percussion layer (e.g. off-beat shaker), the lead, pad switching to the ostinato's harmony.","Notes","A reveal checklist."),
  ("In the drops before the reveal, keep the bass on one note and low-cut it at times so focus stays on the ostinato.","Bass part + EQ automation","Tension without spending the bassline."),
  ("In full sections solo-check each channel and mute anything not needed; automate the constant pad from about -3 dB down to about -14 dB.","Mixer automation","Drops lean on kick, bass and one melody."),
  ("Mark every entry or exit with a small FX hit, drum fill or (in the intro) an orchestral hit.","FX/fill track","Listeners notice each change."),
  ("Before muting many channels at once, insert a short moment of silence.","Arrangement","A clean break that makes large mutes feel intentional."),
  ("At the reveal point change the ostinato for the first time, start the moving bassline, add the new percussion layer and the lead, and move the pad to the ostinato's harmony, all on the same downbeat.","Arrangement","A single goosebump moment where several firsts coincide."),
  ("In the following drop let the bass stop briefly while hats keep running; then switch one element every 8 bars (add, remove, or octave shift).","Arrangement","Momentum continues after the reveal."),
  ("Build down slowly to a loopable outro; keep it shorter for album versions.","Arrangement","An outro that can be looped or ended.")],
 ["The ostinato's notes are identical from its first bar until the reveal bar.","At least three held-back elements first appear on the same bar.","Pad gain automation is about 10 dB lower in full sections than in sparse ones."],
 ["arrangement","song-structure","reveal","ostinato","melodic-techno","breakdown","gain-staging","loopable-intro"],A)
